\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Setup and Notation}
\label{sec:notation}

Let $A$ denote a true $K$-class categorical variable (e.g., sentiment with $K{=}3$ levels) and $A^*$ its observed, potentially misclassified version produced by an LLM annotator. The misclassification process is characterized by a $K{\times}K$ confusion matrix $C$ with entries $C_{jk} = P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k)$, where each column sums to one. Following \citet{hernan_cole2009}, we represent this measurement process using \emph{starred-node} DAGs: the true variable $A$ is latent, and the observed $A^*$ is connected to $A$ via a dashed measurement edge.

\begin{definition}[Non-differential misclassification]
\label{def:nondiff}
Misclassification of $A$ is non-differential with respect to outcome $Y$ and covariates $\mathbf{Z}$ if $P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k, Y, \mathbf{Z}) = P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k) = C_{jk}$ for all $j,k$.
\end{definition}

This assumption is natural when the LLM annotates treatment labels from source documents without access to outcome information, though it may be violated by outcome-conditional prompting or few-shot demonstrations encoding outcome associations (see Limitations).

We study seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy})~\cite{pearl2009} in which $A$ occupies structurally distinct causal roles: confounder, exposure, mediator, collider, M-bias variable, front-door mediator, and instrument. Each role induces a different functional relationship between $C$ and the probability limit of the standard causal estimator, yielding topology-dependent bias.

\subsection{Multi-Class Bias Expressions}
\label{sec:bias_expressions}

For each topology, we derive the probability limit of the naive estimator that substitutes $A^*$ for $A$. Let $\tau$ denote the target causal parameter and $\hat{\tau}(A^*)$ the estimator using misclassified annotations.

\paragraph{Confounding ($A$ = confounder).}
When $A$ confounds the $X{\to}Y$ relationship, the OLS estimator controlling for $A^*$ has probability limit
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:confounding}
\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}(A^*) = \bigl(\mathbb{E}[Z^{\prime}Z]\bigr)^{-1}\mathbb{E}[Z^{\prime}X]\,\boldsymbol{\beta},
\end{equation}
where $Z = [X, \text{dummies}(A^*)]$, $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ contains the true coefficients, and the target parameter $\tau$ is the coefficient on $X$. Misclassification produces residual confounding that monotonically approaches the omitted variable bias, but the sign of $\hat{\tau}$ is preserved: $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$ structurally.\footnote{This result is consistent with \citet{brenner1993}, who showed that the adjusted estimate with misclassified polytomous confounders can be biased toward or away from the null. Both findings hold simultaneously: misclassification degrades confounding control, producing residual bias whose magnitude can exceed the correctly adjusted estimate (amplification), but the treatment effect sign is never reversed. The distinction is between \emph{sign reversal} ($\text{sign}(\hat{\tau}) \neq \text{sign}(\tau)$, which we show never occurs) and \emph{amplification} ($|\hat{\tau}| > |\tau|$, which occurs universally in this topology).}

\paragraph{Exposure ($A$ = treatment variable).}
When $A$ is the exposure of interest, the biased coefficient for category $j$ becomes a weighted combination of true coefficients:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:exposure}
\text{plim}\;\hat{\beta}_j(A^*) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} w_{jk}\,\beta_k,
\end{equation}
where $w_{jk}$ depends on the confusion matrix $C$ and the marginal distribution of $A$. Unlike the binary case, where non-differential misclassification guarantees attenuation toward the null~\cite{brenner1993}, the $K{\geq}3$ case permits $w_{jk}$ to assign negative weight to categories with opposite-sign coefficients, producing \emph{sign reversal}. A near-permutation confusion matrix effectively swaps category labels, making sign reversal the dominant outcome (83.4\% under uniform Dirichlet prior). This failure mode is structurally impossible at $K{=}2$ because a $2{\times}2$ stochastic matrix with positive diagonal cannot reverse the sign of a single coefficient.

\paragraph{Mediation ($A$ = mediator).}
When $A$ mediates the $X{\to}Y$ relationship, the direct effect estimator controlling for $A^*$ has probability limit
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mediation}
\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}_{\text{direct}}(A^*) = \tau_{\text{direct}} + \bigl(1 - \lambda(C)\bigr)\,\tau_{\text{indirect}},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda(C) \in [0,1]$ under proportional effects (Appendix~\ref{app:proof}, Proposition~\ref{prop:lambda_bound}), with $\lambda(I) = 1$ under perfect classification. Misclassification ($\lambda < 1$) causes the residual indirect effect to be absorbed into the direct path estimate, producing upward bias in $|\hat{\tau}_{\text{direct}}|$ but preserving the sign ($\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$).

\paragraph{Collider ($A$ = collider, conditioned on $A^*$).}
Conditioning on a collider $A$ of $X$ and $Y$ opens a spurious path. When we condition on $A^*$ instead, misclassification attenuates this spurious association because $A^*$ is a noisy proxy for $A$. The collider topology thus exhibits a \emph{protective} property: annotation error reduces rather than amplifies the bias from collider stratification, with $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$.

\paragraph{M-bias ($A$ = intermediate collider).}
In the M-shaped DAG ($U_1 \to X$, $U_1 \to A$, $U_2 \to A$, $U_2 \to Y$, $X \to Y$), adjusting for $A$ introduces a small spurious association via the M-bias path~\cite{greenland2003}. Misclassifying $A$ weakens this adjustment, reducing the M-bias. This topology is \emph{immune}: $\hat{\tau}(A^*)$ is closer to $\tau$ than $\hat{\tau}(A)$ for all non-trivial confusion matrices, yielding $\text{ASV} = \infty$.

\paragraph{Front-door ($A$ = front-door mediator).}
In the front-door DAG ($X \to A \to Y$ with unmeasured $U \to X$, $U \to Y$), the front-door estimator~\cite{bellemare_bloem2024} uses $A$ to identify effects despite unmeasured confounding. Misclassification attenuates both $X \to A$ and $A \to Y$ relationships, producing moderate bias with $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$.

\paragraph{Instrumental variable ($A$ = instrument).}
The Wald estimator~\cite{angrist_imbens1995} $\hat{\tau}_{\text{IV}} = \hat{\text{cov}}(A^*, Y) / \hat{\text{cov}}(A^*, X)$ has $C$ in the denominator. Misclassification can drive first-stage coefficients toward zero or reverse their sign, causing the ratio estimator to diverge or flip. Under random Dirichlet matrices, 52.6\% produce sign reversal, making IV the most fragile topology.

\paragraph{Structural sign-flip guarantee.}
\begin{proposition}[Sign preservation---analytic results]
\label{prop:signflip}
Under non-differential misclassification with $C_{kk} > 0$ for all $k$:
\emph{(a)}~Confounding preserves sign when $\operatorname{sign}(\tau) = \operatorname{sign}(\tau {+} B_{\mathrm{OV}})$, where $B_{\mathrm{OV}} {=} \operatorname{Cov}(g(A),f(A))/\operatorname{Var}(T)$ is the omitted-variable bias (Cauchy--Schwarz; Appendix~\ref{app:confounding}).
\emph{(b)}~Collider preserves sign when $|\tau| > \sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(g)\operatorname{Var}(h)}/\mathbb{E}[\operatorname{Var}(T{\mid}A)]$ (Berkson attenuation; Appendix~\ref{app:collider}).
\emph{(c)}~Exposure can produce sign reversal for $K {\geq} 3$, even with $C_{kk} {>} 0.5$ (counterexample in Appendix~\ref{app:exposure}).
\end{proposition}

Our theoretical results establish a three-tier hierarchy: (i)~analytically proved (Proposition~\ref{prop:signflip}(a,b), Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone}), (ii)~numerically verified with zero violations across 100{,}000 matrices (mediation, M-bias, front-door sign preservation), and (iii)~conjectured with $\varepsilon$-approximate bounds (Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}).

\paragraph{Numerical Verification 1} (Mediation, M-bias, front-door).
Sign preservation holds under topology-specific conditions: mediation requires $\operatorname{sign}(\tau_{\mathrm{dir}}) {=} \operatorname{sign}(\tau_{\mathrm{total}})$; front-door requires $\operatorname{sign}(\lambda\alpha_U) {=} \operatorname{sign}(\lambda\alpha_U {+} \operatorname{Cov}(T,f(A)))$; M-bias is attenuating for all $C$. Verified across 100{,}000 random matrices with zero violations (Appendix~\ref{app:conditions}); analytic proofs for general $K$ remain open.

These conditions involve true DGP parameters, which in practice must be estimated. The ASV framework (§\ref{sec:asv}) provides a complementary assessment that does not require knowledge of the exact sufficient conditions.

\subsection{Annotation Sensitivity Values}
\label{sec:asv}

Our bias taxonomy and FWL invariance results naturally motivate a practical question: how much annotation error can a study tolerate before conclusions change? We define Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs) for this purpose, drawing on the E-value framework~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017} but with three key differences: (1)~E-values parametrize a scalar $\Gamma$ that is naturally monotone; ASVs map a $K{\times}K$ matrix to a 1D path $C(\delta)$, requiring proof of $\varepsilon$-approximate monotonicity (Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone}); (2)~three ASV types (magnitude, significance, sign-flip) address topology-dependent failure modes; and (3)~ASVs combine with FWL invariance to route practitioners to topology-appropriate corrections.

\paragraph{Parameterization.}
We parameterize the confusion matrix as $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$, where $C_0$ is a reference confusion matrix (e.g., estimated from a validation set) and $\delta \geq 0$ controls the severity of misclassification. At $\delta{=}0$, classification is perfect; at $\delta{=}1$, classification matches $C_0$. Non-negativity of $C(\delta)$ requires $\delta \leq \delta_{\max} = \min_j \frac{1}{1 - (C_0)_{jj}}$. Since $(C_0)_{jj} \in [0,1]$, we have $\delta_{\max} \geq 1$ for all valid $C_0$, ensuring the full range $[0,1]$ is always feasible; all ASV computations are constrained to $[0, \delta_{\max}]$.
\paragraph{Three ASV types.}
We define three complementary measures: (1)~\textbf{Magnitude ASV} $\delta^*_{\text{mag}} = \min\{\delta : |\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}(C(\delta)) - \tau| > \epsilon\}$---analogous to E-values; (2)~\textbf{Significance ASV} $\delta^*_{\text{sig}} = \min\{\delta : |\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}(C(\delta))| < z_{\alpha/2} \cdot SE(N)\}$---relevant for attenuating topologies; (3)~\textbf{Sign-flip ASV} $\delta^*_{\text{sf}} = \min\{\delta : \text{sign}(\hat{\tau}) \neq \text{sign}(\tau)\}$---exposure and IV only. The significance ASV depends on $N$ through $SE(N)$, exhibiting a sharp phase transition at a critical $N^*$ below which all rejections are blocked.

\begin{theorem}[Envelope monotonicity---confounding and mediation]
\label{thm:monotone}
For confounding and mediation topologies with proportional effects ($f = \lambda g$ for some scalar $\lambda$) under $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$, $|B(C(\delta))|$ is monotonically non-decreasing in $\delta \in [0, \delta_{\max}]$.
\end{theorem}

\emph{Proof sketch.} Both topologies reduce to $B(C(\delta)) {=} \lambda V_W(\delta)/(V_W(\delta) {+} \sigma^2)$ where $V_W(\delta) {=} \operatorname{Var}(g) {-} \operatorname{Var}(\mathbb{E}[g{\mid}A^*_\delta])$ and $\sigma^2$ is a topology-specific constant ($\sigma_T^2$ for confounding, $\mathbb{E}[\operatorname{Var}(T{\mid}A)]$ for mediation). As $\delta$ increases, $A^*$ becomes a noisier channel for $A$, so $\operatorname{Var}(\mathbb{E}[g{\mid}A^*_\delta])$ decreases by the data processing inequality, increasing $V_W$. Since $x/(x{+}c)$ is increasing for $c{>}0$, $|B|$ is non-decreasing (full proofs in Appendix~\ref{app:monotonicity_proof} and~\ref{par:med_monotone}). For general (non-proportional) effects, numerical verification across 10{,}000 random matrices confirms $\varepsilon$-approximate monotonicity with $\varepsilon < 0.02$ (Appendix~\ref{app:monotonicity_proof}).

\begin{conjecture}[$\varepsilon$-approximate envelope monotonicity]
\label{conj:monotone}
For collider, M-bias, and front-door topologies under $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$: collider and M-bias (protective topologies) are $\varepsilon$-approximately non-increasing in $\delta$, and front-door is $\varepsilon$-approximately non-decreasing, with $\varepsilon < 0.06$ ($<$6\% of $\tau$). For exposure, $\varepsilon < 0.008$ at the 99th percentile, though extreme-tail matrices ($<$0.02\% of Dirichlet draws) can reach $\varepsilon \approx 0.15$. Mediation envelope monotonicity is proved for proportional effects (Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone}) and verified numerically for the general case with $\varepsilon < 0.02$. Verified across 30{,}000 random matrices per topology, $K \in \{3,5,7\}$, and $\tau \in \{0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\}$ using analytic probability limits (Appendix~\ref{sec:conjecture_verify}).
\end{conjecture}

The IV topology is excluded from Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}: the Wald estimator exhibits singularities when the first-stage coefficient passes through zero, producing unbounded deviations. Magnitude ASV remains well-defined away from these singularities.

When strict monotonicity holds only approximately ($\varepsilon < 0.06$), the reported $\delta^*$ may differ from the true threshold by at most $\varepsilon/\eta$ in $\delta$-units---negligible at the recommended $\eta = 10\%$. Topology rankings are preserved across all tested configurations.

Binarization of $K{=}3$ annotations introduces additional topology-dependent bias (Appendix~\ref{app:binarization}).

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large language models are rapidly replacing human annotators in computational social science, with researchers using LLM-generated labels for tasks ranging from sentiment classification to political stance detection~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt, ziems2024can, egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, baumann2025}. Crucially, most of these tasks are inherently multi-class: stance detection uses 3--4 categories, sentiment analysis 5, and disaster response taxonomies 10 or more. This shift promises dramatic cost reduction, but introduces a critical validity gap: annotation errors from imperfect classifiers propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the error structure and the causal design. \citet{baumann2025} demonstrate that across 37 annotation tasks, 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with state-of-the-art LLMs, rising to 50\% for smaller models; these errors are highly correlated across models (60\% agreement when both err)~\cite{kim_garg2025}, undermining ensemble-based mitigation. Analyzing the \emph{structure} of annotation error, rather than simply reducing its magnitude, is essential for valid downstream inference.

The transition at $K{\geq}3$ is not gradual but structural. For $K{=}2$, the stochastic constraint forces any confusion matrix with $C_{kk} {>} 0.5$ to preserve coefficient signs---an algebraic property of the $2{\times}2$ matrix dimension, not of a specific error rate~\cite{brenner1993}. This guarantee \emph{vanishes} at $K{\geq}3$: the higher-dimensional confusion matrix admits negative effective weights on categories with opposite-sign coefficients, enabling sign reversal even when every diagonal entry exceeds $0.5$~\cite{veirod_laake2001}. This structural break is quantifiable: under a uniform Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior spanning the full parameter space, 83.4\% of $K{=}3$ exposure confusion matrices produce sign reversal of at least one secondary coefficient (0\% at $K{=}2$); under realistic accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), 2.1\% do so. Critically, the primary treatment coefficient is never reversed---a structural property we prove in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

Standard classification metrics do not predict downstream causal risk: within a narrow $F_1$ range of 0.614--0.690 across three frontier LLMs, relative bias varies by an order of magnitude across topologies (from 4.1\% to 42.1\%), illustrating that \emph{topology}, not annotator quality, determines downstream risk. This fundamental asymmetry has received limited systematic treatment. \citet{dosemeci1990} and \citet{veirod_laake2001} noted the possibility of polytomous sign reversal; \citet{brenner1993} identified amplification for polytomous confounders; \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended classifier error analysis to categorical variables and LLM classifiers, but covered only one DAG topology. No prior work systematically characterizes how the \emph{same} confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across causal designs.

We address these gaps with three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Multi-class bias taxonomy.} We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy}) under $K$-class non-differential misclassification, prove sign preservation for confounding and collider, verify it numerically for mediation, M-bias, and front-door, and show that exposure and IV can produce sign reversal. Binarization amplifies the gap: collapsing $K{=}3$ to binary introduces additional bias varying 154$\times$ across topologies.

\item \textbf{Topology-aware correction guidance.} We evaluate five correction methods and prove a Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance result: matrix-based corrections achieve exactly 0\% bias reduction for four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate---a negative result overlooked in the annotation correction literature. PPI++ achieves 47.0\% RMSE reduction but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%).

\item \textbf{Annotation Sensitivity Values.} As a corollary of the bias taxonomy, we define three complementary ASV types---magnitude, significance, and sign-flip---revealing a topology-dependent vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile, 52.6\% sign reversal) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We characterized how multi-class annotation errors interact with causal DAG topology across seven canonical topologies. The classical binary guarantee breaks at $K \geq 3$: exposure and IV topologies produce sign reversal, while five topologies maintain a structural guarantee. The ASV framework reveals a vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile) to M-bias (immune), and matrix-based corrections are provably ineffective for four topologies due to FWL invariance. Empirical validation with 23 real LLM configurations confirms: the primary treatment effect never reverses, but 61\% of exposure configurations reverse a secondary coefficient. Differential misclassification and correlated multi-LLM errors are natural extensions. Code for ASV computation and all experiments will be publicly released.

\section*{Limitations}

Our framework assumes non-differential misclassification ($P(A^*|A, Y, \mathbf{Z}) = P(A^*|A)$), which holds when the LLM does not observe $Y$ but may be violated when it accesses $Y$-correlated information---e.g., outcome-conditional prompting, few-shot demonstrations encoding outcome--treatment associations, or retrieval-augmented annotation carrying outcome signal (Appendix~\ref{sec:nondiff_stress}). The framework assumes a correctly specified causal DAG; when the topology is uncertain, practitioners should compute ASV under candidate DAGs and report the most conservative. Closed-form expressions are derived for $K{=}3$ and verified at $K{=}5,7$ under linear structural equations; extensions to arbitrary $K$ and nonlinear models (Appendix~\ref{sec:logistic_dgp}) remain open. We address misclassification of $A$ by a single annotator; outcome misclassification and correlated multi-annotator settings~\cite{kim_garg2025} introduce additional bias channels not captured here.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Measurement error in causal inference.}
The foundations are established in \citet{carroll2006}, \citet{gustafson2004}, \citet{buonaccorsi2010}, and \citet{bound2001measurement}, with starred-node DAGs formalized by \citet{hernan_cole2009}. For binary variables, non-differential misclassification biases toward the null~\cite{brenner1993, ogburn_vanderweele2012}, but this fails for polytomous exposures~\cite{dosemeci1990, jurek2005, veirod_laake2001}. \citet{edwards_lessler_naimi2021} showed dependent misclassification can bias in either direction, a concern that also drives mediation analyses with mismeasured mediators~\cite{valeri_vanderweele2013}. All analyze a single DAG topology; ours is the first to characterize how the \emph{same} confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across seven topologies.

\paragraph{Text classifiers and LLM annotation.}
\citet{grimmer2022} and \citet{feder_etal2022} provide foundations for text-as-data and causal inference in NLP. \citet{wood_doughty2018} first applied DAG reasoning to classifier error; \citet{teblunthuis2024} gave the most complete binary treatment ($K{=}2$, four DAGs); \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended to categorical LLM classifiers but covered only confounding. On annotation quality, \citet{gilardi2023chatgpt} and \citet{ziems2024can} document the rapid adoption of LLM annotators, \citet{baumann2025} found 31\% incorrect conclusions, \citet{pangakis_wolken_fasching2023} demonstrated that automated annotations require task-specific validation, \citet{cheng_mayya_sedoc2024} propose structured approaches to reduce LLM measurement error, and \citet{kim_garg2025} showed 60\% error correlation across models. \citet{audinet2025} benchmark debiasing; \citet{keith_jensen_oconnor2020} survey the broader landscape. These works either restrict to binary labels, cover a single topology, or diagnose quality without connecting it to causal validity.

\paragraph{Sensitivity analysis and correction methods.}
Our ASV framework draws on E-values~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017}, the robustness value of \citet{cinelli2020making}, and the mediation sensitivity analysis of \citet{imai_keele_yamamoto2010}, but parametrizes annotation error severity rather than omitted variable strength or sequential ignorability violations. The unified measurement-error and missing-data framework of \citet{blackwell_honaker_king2017} similarly motivates treating annotation noise as a first-class object of inference. For correction, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023} and DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023} are topology-agnostic; \citet{zrnic_candes2024} extend PPI to gold-free settings, and \citet{gligoric2025} leverage LLM confidence scores. Our contribution reveals that this agnosticism is costly: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent, with matrix-based methods provably ineffective for four topologies. Bayesian measurement error models~\cite{richardson1993bayesian, gustafson2004} and EM imputation offer alternatives that bypass FWL invariance.

